    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "p1",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Godzilla, the "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "destructive",
            "ref": "v-destructive"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " monster, has been "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "stomping",
            "zh": "重步走"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " across the big screen for years. Though many recent Godzilla movies were made in the U.S., we have a Japanese director to thank for this amazing creature. He was Eiji Tsuburaya."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p2",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Tsuburaya was born in Tokyo in 1901. He grew up in a family of "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "grocery distributors",
            "zh": "经销商"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ". From a young age, the boy had been known for his talent for making model airplanes. Local newspapers called him a “child "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "craftsman",
            "ref": "v-craftsman"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "”."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p3",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "At that time, the movie industry had just begun. Tsuburaya was quite interested in it. In 1919, he broke into the industry and served as a cameraman. His "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "fame",
            "ref": "v-fame"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " grew slowly as he kept inventing moviemaking tools using his quick brain."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p4",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "In 1933, the American monster movie "
          },
          {
            "t": "em",
            "v": "King Kong"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " hit Japanese theatres. The movie’s special effects opened up a new world of possibilities for Tsuburaya. He carefully studied the movie again and again."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p5",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Later, inspired by real-life events, Tsuburaya and movie producer Tomoyuki Tanaka wanted to make a movie about a "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "radiation-affected",
            "zh": "辐射影响的"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " monster. After much discussion, that monster became Godzilla."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p6",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Tsuburaya wanted to create something truly different—and truly amazing. He built a mini “Tokyo” and put actors in Godzilla "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "costumes",
            "ref": "v-costume"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " to destroy it. Shooting the movie was tiring. One actor, Haruo Nakajima, lost 10 kg in the process. But Godzilla was a hit. Almost everybody in Japan was talking excitedly about it."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p7",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Tsuburaya didn’t stop there. He went on to improve his special effects and created many more works. Though Tsuburaya died in 1970, his creations have remained popular to this day. Godzilla is still the first name that "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "pops up",
            "ref": "v-pop-up"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " in our minds when we talk about on-screen monsters today."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
